# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values.
# .env.local is gitignored — never commit Supabase keys or the dev password.

# --- Supabase (dev project) — Project Settings → API ---
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-or-publishable-key

# --- Dev auto-login shim (docs/auth-plan.md §1) — DEV BUILDS ONLY ---
# While auth screens are deferred, `npm run dev` can silently sign in as the
# seeded dev user so the whole auth pipeline (sessions, auth.uid(), RLS) is live.
#
# Set VITE_DEV_AUTOLOGIN=true to enable it, and seed the dev user first
# (see supabase/dev-user.sql). VITE_DEV_USER_EMAIL / _PASSWORD must match the
# credentials you gave that user.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED: a production build (`npm run build`) with VITE_DEV_AUTOLOGIN=true
# refuses to build, and the runtime shim refuses to start. Leave it false/unset
# for anything that ships.
VITE_DEV_AUTOLOGIN=false
VITE_DEV_USER_EMAIL=dev@euro28.local
VITE_DEV_USER_PASSWORD=set-this-to-match-the-dashboard-user

# --- Cloudflare Turnstile (bot protection on auth) — OFF by default ---
# PUBLIC Turnstile site key. When set, the log-in / sign-up forms render the
# Turnstile widget and send a captchaToken to Supabase Auth. LEAVE UNSET to keep
# auth exactly as-is. You must ALSO enable Turnstile in the Supabase dashboard
# (Authentication → CAPTCHA) with the matching SECRET — the two move together
# (a token while CAPTCHA is disabled, or CAPTCHA enabled without a token, both
# error). The secret never lives in this repo; Supabase holds it and runs
# siteverify. In Netlify, add VITE_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY to the site's env vars too.
VITE_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# Dev auto-login is headless (no widget). If you enable CAPTCHA on the DEV
# Supabase project, configure it with Cloudflare's always-passes TEST secret
# (1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA) and set any dummy value here so startup
# sign-in still passes. For a real-checkbox dev experience, use Cloudflare's TEST
# site key (1x00000000000000000000AA) as VITE_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY. Leave blank off.
VITE_TURNSTILE_DEV_TOKEN=

# --- User support (private Account page) ---
# Optional public contact address used only to build a mailto link. When blank
# the Account page shows an honest unavailable state instead of a dead action.
VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL=

# --- Sentry error reporting (privacy-restricted) — OFF by default ---
# Operational detail and the approved DSN live in docs/ops-sentry.md; this block
# exists so the template stays a complete list of what the app reads.
#
# FAIL-QUIET, not fail-closed: every invalid combination below disables reporting
# and warns in development rather than throwing, so a misconfiguration never
# stops the app from starting — but it also never surfaces at runtime. Check the
# dev console if events do not arrive.
#
# Must be exactly the string "true" to enable. Any other value, including "1"
# and "TRUE", leaves Sentry off.
VITE_SENTRY_ENABLED=false
# Required when enabled, and PUBLIC (a browser DSN, never the auth token). Must
# be an https DSN on an approved ingest host — *.ingest.sentry.io,
# *.ingest.us.sentry.io or *.ingest.de.sentry.io. Those hosts are also listed in
# netlify.toml's CSP connect-src, and the two are kept in step by
# tests/scripts/contentSecurityPolicyParity.test.ts: adding a region to one side
# only means the browser blocks the envelope with no application error.
# A missing or unparseable DSN disables reporting even when enabled above.
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=
# Set to "true" to emit one synthetic verification event at startup, to confirm
# the SDK reaches the project. Ignored in production builds by design, so it can
# never add noise to real error data.
VITE_SENTRY_VERIFICATION_EVENT=false

# --- UI migration route flags (src/app/routeFlags.ts) ---
# Selects the next-generation season Match Predictor journey. Fails closed: only
# the exact string "true" switches it on, so an unset or misspelled value leaves
# the legacy route serving players. Vite inlines this at build time, so the
# rollback is a rebuild rather than a live toggle.
VITE_UI_SEASON_MATCH_PREDICTOR=false

# Serves the public landing page (modernisation plan Appendix E) at `/` to a
# signed-out visitor. Off, `/` redirects to `/auth/login` exactly as it did
# before the page existed — so the rollback is the previous behaviour rather
# than a second implementation kept alive alongside it.
VITE_UI_PUBLIC_LANDING=false

# --- Official club badges (ADR 0017, src/app/clubBadgePolicy.ts) ---
# OFF, AND NOT MERELY UNCONFIGURED. ADR 0017 decided this product launches
# badge-free: a club crest is simultaneously a registered trade mark and an
# original artistic work, and the 8 August 2026 provider capability audit found
# that all four configured providers serve an image and disclaim the rights to
# it. A URL in a payload is not a licence.
#
# The capability exists so the option stays open, and turning it on takes three
# deliberate changes rather than one: a recorded rights decision, a provider
# adapter that actually carries a badge field (none does today), and an
# `img-src` in netlify.toml that admits the provider host — it is `'self' data:`
# today and would block every remote badge regardless of this flag.
VITE_UI_OFFICIAL_BADGES=false
# Approved providers, comma-separated: football-data, sportmonks, api-sports.
# Empty approves nobody, which is what the flag above already means.
VITE_UI_OFFICIAL_BADGE_SOURCES=
# Season ids allowed to show badges, comma-separated. Unset means every
# competition once the switches above allow it; naming any narrows it to those,
# because rights and provider art quality are not uniform across competitions.
VITE_UI_OFFICIAL_BADGE_COMPETITIONS=

# --- Stage 14, the Football Hub cutover (src/app/routeFlags.ts) ---
# ON. `config/vnext-programme.json` carries `productionCutoverAuthorized: true`
# and all three hosted environments are level at Contract 208, so the reads
# these surfaces make exist everywhere they run.
#
# ONE FLAG PER DESTINATION, NOT ONE FOR THE HUB, because the stage contract asks
# for a staged deployment and rollback plan and an all-or-nothing switch is
# neither: it cannot be advanced one surface at a time, and rolling it back
# withdraws surfaces that were fine along with the one that was not.
#
# Every legacy route component is STILL MOUNTED on the off branch. Setting any
# one of these to anything but `true` restores yesterday's journey for that one
# destination, immediately, with no data rollback.
VITE_UI_FOOTBALL_HUB_HOME=true
VITE_UI_FOOTBALL_HUB_MATCHES=true
VITE_UI_FOOTBALL_HUB_GAMES=true
VITE_UI_FOOTBALL_HUB_LEAGUES=true
VITE_UI_FOOTBALL_HUB_PLAYER_PROFILE=true
VITE_UI_FOOTBALL_HUB_DISCOVERY=true
VITE_UI_FOOTBALL_HUB_ACCOUNT=true
VITE_UI_FOOTBALL_HUB_LMS=true
VITE_UI_FOOTBALL_HUB_CHAMPIONSHIP=true
VITE_UI_FOOTBALL_HUB_PREDICTOR=true

# --- The two deployments (ADR 0026, src/app/site/) ---
# One `main` commit builds two products on two domains: the weekly Football
# Prediction Hub and the Euro 2028 Predictor. This variable selects which.
#
# FAILS CLOSED TOWARDS THE HUB. Only the exact value "euro" (trimmed and
# lower-cased) builds the Euro site; unset, misspelled or unknown builds the
# Hub. That direction is deliberate: `EURO-001` requires Euro 2028 to stay
# invisible until an owner publishes it, so a mistyped variable must produce
# the weekly platform rather than a tournament nobody approved. Whether Euro is
# actually visible remains the server's decision (`euro_publication_state`).
VITE_SITE_VARIANT=hub

# This deployment's own absolute origin, e.g. https://euro28predictor.com.
# It builds the canonical URL, the Open Graph and Twitter URLs, the generated
# sitemap.xml and the Sitemap: line in robots.txt.
#
# Leaving it blank is supported for an intentionally unaddressed local build.
# Hosted sites should set it explicitly; both Netlify production projects do.
VITE_PUBLIC_SITE_ORIGIN=

# The OTHER deployment's absolute origin, so each site can offer a link to its
# sibling ("keep playing on the Hub", "the Euro game lives here"). Blank omits
# the link rather than rendering a dead one.
VITE_SIBLING_SITE_ORIGIN=

# --- Product analytics (PostHog) — OFF by default ---
# PUBLIC project key only. Leaving this blank means the PostHog chunk is not
# loaded and no product analytics events are sent. Automatic capture is off.
VITE_POSTHOG_KEY=
# Public ingest host for the PostHog project. No private AI/provider data.
VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# --- Notification delivery (Novu) — SERVER-SIDE ONLY, OFF by default ---
# These are deliberately NOT VITE_-prefixed and must never become so. Vite
# inlines every VITE_ variable into the browser bundle, and NOVU_API_KEY is a
# secret key with full environment authority — in a browser it would be
# readable by every visitor. They are read only by server-side code through
# src/services/notifications/, never through import.meta.env, and
# tests/services/notifications/notificationBoundary.test.ts fails if that
# changes.
#
# FAILS CLOSED, and needs BOTH switches. A credential on its own delivers
# nothing: a key that happens to be in scope on a preview, a CI runner or a
# developer machine is not consent to send real notifications to real players.
#
# The Novu secret key. Leave BLANK here — it belongs in the deployment's own
# secret store, never in a committed file.
NOVU_API_KEY=
# Must be the word "enabled" to send. Anything else — unset, "true", "1",
# "yes" — leaves delivery off. Case and surrounding whitespace are forgiven.
NOTIFICATIONS_DELIVERY=
# Free-text label recorded alongside delivery attempts, e.g. "staging".
NOTIFICATIONS_ENVIRONMENT=
# Optional. Points the adapter at a self-hosted Novu instead of the Novu cloud
# API. Blank uses https://api.novu.co.
NOVU_API_ORIGIN=
